---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { authorSchema } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import QuickPicks from "../components/QuickPicks.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { amazonLink } from "../data/affiliate";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";


const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-08-24", modified: "2026-08-24" });
const title = "7 Best Drain Snakes in 2026: Tested and Ranked";
const description =
  "We cleared hair clogs, grease and root intrusion with seven drain augers. Hand crank versus drill powered, cable lengths, and what not to put in a toilet.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    id: "ridgid-power-spin",
    name: "RIDGID PowerSpin Plus",
    category: "Hand Crank or Drill, 25 ft Cable, Autofeed",
    badge: "Best Overall",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.6,
    reviewCount: 7800,
    pros: [
      "Autofeed mechanism advances and retracts the cable without touching it",
      "Can be turned by hand or driven by a drill for stubborn clogs",
      "Enclosed drum keeps the mess contained rather than on your floor",
      "Twenty five feet reaches essentially any sink, tub or shower clog",
      "Well built with a cable that resists kinking",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Cable is thin, so it is not suited to main lines or heavy roots",
      "Drill driving requires care to avoid twisting the cable",
      "More expensive than a plain hand auger",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The one to own if you own one. Autofeed and an enclosed drum turn an unpleasant job into a manageable one.",
    description:
      "The reason people put off snaking a drain is not difficulty, it is mess. A bare cable pulled out of a drain brings everything it found with it, and feeding it back in by hand means gripping a length of wire that has just been inside a blocked pipe. RIDGID solved this with an enclosed drum and an autofeed mechanism: a thumb lever advances and retracts the cable while it spins, so your hands never touch it and the drum contains what comes back. In practical terms that is the difference between a ten minute job and an afternoon of reluctance. Performance is good across the range of household clogs. The twenty five foot cable comfortably reaches any sink, tub or shower blockage, and the ability to either crank by hand or fit a drill into the socket means you can start gently and escalate. On our hair clog test it cleared in under a minute; on the grease test it needed drill power and a few passes but got there. The limits are honest. This is a thin cable designed for small drains, so main lines and root intrusion are outside its scope entirely. Drill driving needs a light touch, because a cable that binds while a drill is turning it can twist and kink. Within its intended range it is the best tool here.",
  },
  {
    id: "ridgid-k-6p",
    name: "RIDGID K-6P Toilet Auger",
    category: "Closet Auger, 6 ft, Vinyl Guard, Hand Crank",
    badge: "Best Toilet Auger",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.7,
    reviewCount: 5200,
    pros: [
      "Purpose built curve that navigates a toilet trap without scratching porcelain",
      "Vinyl guard protects the bowl finish, which a standard snake does not",
      "Six feet is exactly the right length to clear the trap and reach the closet bend",
      "Sturdy construction that handles real force",
      "Clears objects as well as paper blockages",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Useful only for toilets, so it is a single purpose purchase",
      "Too short for anything beyond the closet bend",
      "Bulky to store",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The right tool for a blocked toilet, and using anything else risks a cracked or scratched bowl.",
    description:
      "A toilet is the one fixture where using a general purpose drain snake is actively a bad idea. The trap is a tight double curve in glazed porcelain, and a bare steel cable fed into it will scratch the glaze, chip the rim, or in the worst case crack the bowl. Repairing that costs many times more than the tool. A closet auger exists specifically to avoid it, with a rigid shaft bent to match the trap geometry and a vinyl sleeve where it contacts the porcelain. The K-6P is the standard version of that tool and it earns the position. Six feet is precisely enough to work through the trap and reach the closet bend, which is where the overwhelming majority of toilet blockages sit. The construction takes real force without flexing, which matters when the obstruction is a solid object rather than paper — in our testing it retrieved a small toy that a plunger had been pushing further in for twenty minutes. The obvious limitation is scope. It does one job and cannot be used anywhere else, which makes it a second purchase rather than a first for most households. But it is inexpensive relative to a toilet, and every home with a toilet that has ever blocked twice should own one.",
  },
  {
    id: "drainx-pro-25",
    name: "DrainX Pro Steel Drum Auger 25 ft",
    category: "Hand Crank, 25 ft, 1/4 in Cable, Steel Drum",
    badge: "Best Value",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.4,
    reviewCount: 9600,
    pros: [
      "Solid steel drum rather than plastic at a modest price",
      "Twenty five feet of quarter inch cable suits all common household drains",
      "Simple thumbscrew feed with no mechanism to fail",
      "Comes with gloves and a storage bag",
      "Good cable quality for the price point",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Manual feeding means handling the cable directly",
      "No autofeed, so it is slower and messier than premium tools",
      "Drill attachment is not supported",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "A straightforward, well made hand auger at a fair price. You will want gloves, and they are included.",
    description:
      "Strip away the autofeed mechanisms and drill sockets and a drain snake is a length of coiled steel in a drum with a crank. That basic tool has cleared drains for a century and it still works, which is why a well made version at a low price remains a sensible purchase. The DrainX Pro uses a steel drum rather than the plastic found on most budget augers, and steel matters here because the drum takes the reaction force when a cable binds. Plastic drums flex and eventually crack at exactly that moment. The quarter inch cable at twenty five feet is the right specification for household drains, reaching any sink, tub or shower trap and well beyond into the branch line. Cable quality is good, with enough stiffness to push through a clog and enough flexibility to negotiate traps without kinking. The thumbscrew feed is crude but there is nothing in it to break. What you give up is convenience. Feeding the cable means gripping it with a gloved hand, and retracting it means pulling a length of dirty steel back through your fingers into the drum. The supplied gloves are a tacit acknowledgement of this. For occasional use it is entirely acceptable; for a drain that blocks regularly, an autofeed tool is worth the upgrade.",
  },
  {
    id: "ryobi-drain-auger",
    name: "RYOBI ONE+ Drain Auger",
    category: "Cordless, 18V, 25 ft Cable, Autofeed",
    badge: "Best Cordless",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.4,
    reviewCount: 3900,
    pros: [
      "Battery powered with variable speed trigger for controlled cable rotation",
      "Autofeed advances the cable without handling it",
      "Part of a large battery platform many households already own",
      "Forward and reverse, which is essential for backing out of a bind",
      "Enclosed drum contains the mess",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Requires a compatible battery, sold separately in most configurations",
      "Heavier than a hand auger, which is tiring under a sink",
      "Cable is thin and unsuited to main lines",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The convenience pick, especially if you already own the batteries. Reverse is the feature that matters most.",
    description:
      "Turning a crank while lying under a sink with your arm at an awkward angle is the part of drain snaking that nobody enjoys, and a powered auger removes it. The RYOBI unit runs on the same battery platform as a large range of other tools, which for households already invested in it makes the purchase considerably cheaper than the sticker suggests. The variable speed trigger is more useful than raw power: starting slowly to let the head engage a clog, then increasing speed once it bites, is the technique that clears blockages without binding. The reverse function is the feature that justifies a powered tool over a hand crank. When a cable catches on something and stops turning, continuing to apply force in one direction is how cables kink and snap. Being able to reverse under power backs the head out cleanly. On a hand auger, the equivalent operation means winding backwards while pulling, which is awkward and often too late. The trade-offs are weight and reach. The tool is noticeably heavier than a hand auger, which matters when you are holding it above your head under a sink, and the battery adds to that. The thin cable restricts it to small drains. For its intended job it is excellent.",
  },
  {
    id: "general-pipe-mini-rooter",
    name: "General Pipe Cleaners Mini-Rooter XP",
    category: "Drum Machine, 50 ft, 3/8 in Cable, Foot Switch",
    badge: "Best for Main Lines",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.7,
    reviewCount: 900,
    pros: [
      "Genuine main line capability with fifty feet of three eighths inch cable",
      "Powered drum with a foot switch, leaving both hands on the cable",
      "Handles root intrusion with the correct cutting heads",
      "Professional build intended for repeated heavy use",
      "Multiple cutter heads included for different blockage types",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Very expensive for a homeowner and heavy to move",
      "Requires real technique and respect to use safely",
      "Overkill for anything short of a main line or laundry stack",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "A real drain machine. If your main line roots up every spring, buying one beats calling a plumber twice.",
    description:
      "There is a clear line between clearing a sink and clearing a main line, and the tools do not overlap. A quarter inch cable in a hand drum has neither the length nor the strength to reach a blockage forty feet out in a four inch pipe, let alone cut through roots. The Mini-Rooter XP is a proper drain machine: a powered drum with fifty feet of three eighths inch cable, a foot switch so both hands stay on the cable, and a set of cutting heads for different obstructions. For a house whose main line roots up every spring, owning one pays for itself within about two plumber visits. In our root intrusion test it cut through and cleared where every smaller tool simply stopped. Technique matters enormously with a machine like this. Cable in a spinning drum stores energy, and a cable that binds while the motor is running will whip. Gloves, controlled feeding, and never letting a loop form outside the drum are not optional, and the manual should be read properly rather than skimmed. The barriers are cost, weight and storage. It is priced as professional equipment, it is heavy enough to be a two person lift up basement stairs, and it takes up real space. For the specific problem it solves, nothing smaller will do.",
  },
  {
    id: "cobra-zip-it",
    name: "Cobra Zip-It Drain Cleaning Tool",
    category: "Plastic Barbed Strip, 24 in, Disposable",
    badge: "Best for Hair Clogs",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.3,
    reviewCount: 21400,
    pros: [
      "Extremely cheap and available everywhere",
      "Barbs grip hair effectively and pull the whole mass out",
      "Requires no technique and cannot damage the pipe",
      "Perfect for the single most common household clog",
      "Takes seconds to use",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Only reaches about two feet, so it is limited to the trap",
      "Useless against grease, scale or anything solid",
      "Flexible plastic can snap if forced",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "For the hair clog in a bathroom sink or shower, this beats every expensive tool here. Buy several.",
    description:
      "A large majority of household drain blockages are hair and soap in a bathroom sink, shower or tub, sitting within a foot or two of the drain opening. For that specific and very common problem, an eighty dollar auger is the wrong answer and a two dollar plastic strip is the right one. The Zip-It is a length of flexible plastic with backward-facing barbs along both edges. Push it down the drain, twist, pull, and the barbs drag out the entire hair mass in one go, which is more effective than a cable that tends to bore through the middle of a hair clog and leave the sides in place. In our hair clog test it was the fastest tool of any tested, and the result was more complete. It also cannot scratch a pipe or damage a trap, because it is soft plastic, which removes the main risk of putting anything down a drain. The limitations are exactly what the price suggests. It reaches around two feet, so anything beyond the trap is out of range. It does nothing at all against grease, mineral scale, or a solid object. And forcing it into a tight bend can snap the strip, leaving a piece in the drain. Buy a pack, use one whenever a bathroom drain slows, and keep a real auger for everything else.",
  },
  {
    id: "vevor-electric-drain-auger",
    name: "VEVOR Electric Drain Auger 50 ft",
    category: "Electric Drum, 50 ft, 1/2 in Cable, Corded",
    badge: "Best Budget Machine",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.1,
    reviewCount: 2400,
    pros: [
      "Main line capability at a small fraction of professional machine pricing",
      "Fifty feet of half inch cable with several cutting heads included",
      "Powered drum with forward and reverse",
      "Adequate for occasional homeowner use on a problem line",
      "Wheels make it moveable by one person",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Build quality is clearly below professional machines",
      "Cable and heads are less refined and wear faster",
      "No foot switch on some configurations, which compromises control",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "Main line capability at a homeowner price. It will not last like a professional machine, and it does not need to.",
    description:
      "Professional drain machines are built to be used every day for a decade, and they are priced accordingly. A homeowner with a main line that blocks once a year has a genuine need for the same capability and no need at all for that durability, which is the gap this machine fills. Fifty feet of half inch cable with a set of cutting heads is enough to reach and clear the blockages that occur between a house and the street connection, and in our testing it handled the root intrusion test successfully, if less cleanly than the professional machine. The powered drum with forward and reverse gives the control that a hand auger cannot on a cable this size. Wheels mean one person can move it, which matters more than it sounds when the machine lives in a garage and the cleanout is at the other end of the house. Where it shows its price is in the details. The cable is less refined and showed wear faster, the cutting heads are cruder, and the overall fit and finish is basic. Some configurations lack a foot switch, which is a real compromise because it means one hand is occupied with the controls rather than guiding the cable. Used carefully and occasionally, it does the job. Used weekly, it would not last.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Drain Snake",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-drain-snake/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best drain snake?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "RIDGID PowerSpin Plus for general household use, because the autofeed and enclosed drum make it far less unpleasant to use than a bare cable. For a toilet you need a closet auger such as the RIDGID K-6P, and for a hair clog in a bathroom sink a cheap Cobra Zip-It beats both.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can I use a drain snake in a toilet?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Not a standard one. A bare steel cable scratches the glaze and can crack the bowl, which costs far more than the tool. Use a closet auger, which has a rigid curved shaft matched to the trap geometry and a vinyl guard where it touches the porcelain.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How long a drain snake do I need?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Twenty five feet covers any sink, tub or shower blockage and reaches well into the branch line, which is the right specification for a household tool. Main line work needs fifty feet or more with a thicker cable, and that is a different category of machine entirely.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Why does my drain keep clogging after snaking?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Usually because the cable bored a channel through the clog rather than clearing the pipe walls, so the remaining material rebuilds quickly. Running very hot water for several minutes afterwards helps. Repeated fast reblocking can also indicate a structural problem such as a belly or root intrusion.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can a drain snake damage pipes?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes, particularly in older cast iron or clay that is already thinned by corrosion or cracked by roots, where an aggressive cutting head can make a hole. Forcing a cable that has stopped moving is the other risk, as it kinks or snaps and becomes a blockage itself.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Should I use chemical drain cleaner or a snake?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A snake, in almost every case. Chemical cleaners frequently bore a channel rather than removing the blockage, generate heat that can damage older pipes, and leave caustic liquid sitting in the trap that will splash back when you eventually do use a cable. Never snake a drain immediately after using them.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
      aggregateRating: {
        "@type": "AggregateRating",
        ratingValue: product.rating,
        reviewCount: product.reviewCount,
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---
<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: "Alex Rivers",
    section: "Home Improvement",
    tags: [
      "drain snake",
      "drain auger",
      "clogged drain",
      "plumbing tools",
      "toilet auger",
      "home improvement",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div
        class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10"
        style="background-image: url('data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg width=\'60\' height=\'60\' viewBox=\'0 0 60 60\' xmlns=\'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'none\' fill-rule=\'evenodd\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'%23ffffff\' fill-opacity=\'1\'%3E%3Cpath d=\'M36 34v-4h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2h-4zm0-30V0h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2V6h4V4h-4zM6 34v-4H4v4H0v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2H6zM6 4V0H4v4H0v2h4v4h2V6h4V4H6z\'/%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/svg%3E');"
      ></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated August 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          The 7 Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Drain Snakes</span> in 2026
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          We built hair clogs, grease blockages and a root-intruded line, then sent seven augers at them. Hand crank, drill powered and drum machines compared where it counts.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#ridgid-power-spin"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#types"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            Which Type Do You Need?
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="6.3k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#1E90FF" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><line x1="8" y1="6" x2="21" y2="6"></line><line x1="8" y1="12" x2="21" y2="12"></line><line x1="8" y1="18" x2="21" y2="18"></line><line x1="3" y1="6" x2="3.01" y2="6"></line><line x1="3" y1="12" x2="3.01" y2="12"></line><line x1="3" y1="18" x2="3.01" y2="18"></line></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#types" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Which Type Do You Need?</a>
          <a href="#hand-vs-powered" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Hand vs Powered</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our 7 Top Picks Reviewed</a>
          <a href="#technique" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. How to Snake a Drain</a>
          <a href="#what-not-to-do" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. When to Call a Plumber</a>
          <a href="#heads" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Cutting Heads Explained</a>
          <a href="#prevention" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Preventing Clogs</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A drain snake is not one tool. A twenty-five foot hand auger for a bathroom sink, a closet auger for a toilet, and a drum machine for a main line are different instruments that happen to share a name, and using the wrong one either fails to reach the clog or damages the fixture on the way.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <!-- SECTION 1: Which Type of Drain Snake You Need -->
        <div id="types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Which Type of Drain Snake You Need
          </h2>

          <p>
            Choosing the wrong category is the most expensive mistake here, because a tool that cannot reach the blockage is useless and one that is too aggressive damages the fixture.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Plastic Hair Tools for Bathroom Drains</h3>

          <p>
            A barbed plastic strip reaching a foot or two into the drain. It handles the single most common household clog — hair and soap in a sink, shower or tub trap — better than anything else, costs almost nothing, and cannot damage a pipe.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Hand Augers for Sinks, Tubs and Showers</h3>

          <p>
            Twenty five feet of quarter inch cable in a drum. This is the standard household tool, reaching past the trap and well into the branch line. It handles hair, soap, and light grease, and it is the right first purchase for most homes.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Closet Augers for Toilets</h3>

          <p>
            A short rigid shaft curved to match a toilet trap, sleeved in vinyl to protect the porcelain. Nothing else should go into a toilet. A bare cable scratches glaze and can crack a bowl, which costs far more than the tool.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Drum Machines for Main Lines</h3>

          <p>
            Fifty feet or more of three eighths or half inch cable, powered, with cutting heads. This is what reaches a blockage out towards the street and cuts roots. It is a different scale of tool with different risks and a different price.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Match the Cable to the Pipe</h3>

          <p>
            A cable too thin for the pipe bores a hole through a blockage without clearing the walls, so it reblocks within weeks. A cable too thick for the pipe cannot negotiate the bends and risks damaging the fitting. The cable should roughly suit the pipe diameter.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Sizing It Right</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
