                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Right length for sinks and tubs</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">6 ft</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Closet auger length for toilets</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">50 ft+</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">What a main line actually needs</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#FF4500]">1/4 in</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Cable diameter for small drains</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 2: Hand Crank vs Drill Driven vs Powered -->
        <div id="hand-vs-powered" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Hand Crank vs Drill Driven vs Powered
          </h2>

          <p>
            Once you know the type, the second decision is how the cable is turned, and it affects both effectiveness and how much damage a mistake causes.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Hand cranking gives the most feedback.</strong> You feel exactly when the head meets resistance, when it bites, and when it is binding, which makes it the safest way to work and the easiest to learn. It is also slow and tiring on a stubborn clog.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Drill driving multiplies force, including the wrong force.</strong> Fitting a drill to an auger clears blockages a hand crank cannot, and it also means a cable that catches keeps being twisted after you would have stopped. Use a low speed and be ready to release the trigger instantly.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Powered drums are for large cables only.</strong> A dedicated machine has the mass and the controls to manage a heavy cable, and a foot switch keeps both hands guiding it. This is the right approach for main lines and the wrong one for a bathroom sink.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Reverse is the most important control.</strong> A cable that binds needs backing out, not more forward force. Powered tools with reverse handle this cleanly; hand augers require winding backwards while pulling, which is awkward and often applied too late.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Autofeed is worth paying for.</strong> Advancing and retracting the cable with a lever rather than your hands makes the job dramatically less unpleasant, which in practice means it gets done promptly instead of being postponed until the drain stops entirely.
          </p>
        </div>

