        <!-- SECTION 4: Using a Drain Snake Properly -->
        <div id="technique" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Using a Drain Snake Properly
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most failed attempts come down to technique, and the corrections are simple enough to learn in one job.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Remove the trap first where you can.</strong> On a sink, unscrewing the P-trap takes five minutes with a bucket underneath and very often reveals the clog sitting right there. Snaking through a trap you could have removed is doing the job the hard way.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Feed slowly and let the head do the work.</strong> Pushing hard turns a cable into a coiled spring that kinks rather than advances. Feed a short length, turn, feed more. When resistance appears, keep turning at a steady rate rather than shoving.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Turn continuously while advancing and retracting.</strong> A spinning head cuts and grips; a stationary one just pushes. Keeping the cable turning as you pull back is what brings the blockage out rather than leaving it behind.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Do not use a snake after chemical drain cleaner.</strong> Caustic cleaner sitting in the trap will splash back at you when the cable disturbs it. If chemicals have been used, flush thoroughly with water first and wear eye protection regardless.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Run hot water afterwards, for several minutes.</strong> Clearing a passage through a clog is not the same as removing it. A sustained hot flush carries away what the cable loosened, and skipping it is why drains reblock quickly after apparently successful snaking.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Clean the cable before it goes away.</strong> Wiping the cable and running a rag along it as it retracts stops the drum filling with the material that caused the clog, which otherwise dries into a solid mass that seizes the tool.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">Pull the Trap Before You Snake</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              On any sink, take five minutes and a bucket to unscrew the P-trap first. A large share of kitchen and bathroom blockages are sitting in that trap, visible and removable by hand. Snaking through a trap you could have simply removed and rinsed is the most common way people make an easy job difficult.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 5: What Not to Snake and When to Stop -->
        <div id="what-not-to-do" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. What Not to Snake and When to Stop
          </h2>

          <p>
            Some situations get worse with a cable, and knowing them prevents an inconvenience becoming an expense.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Never put a standard cable in a toilet.</strong> The glazed porcelain scratches easily and the bowl can crack under force. A closet auger with a vinyl guard exists specifically for this, and it costs a fraction of a replacement toilet.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Be cautious with old cast iron and clay.</strong> An aggressive cutting head in a pipe already thinned by corrosion or cracked by roots can turn a partial blockage into a hole. If the house is old and the line is original, a camera inspection before cutting is money well spent.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Stop if multiple fixtures are backing up.</strong> A single slow drain is a branch problem. A toilet backing up when the washing machine drains, or water appearing in a shower when a toilet flushes, points to the main line, which is a different job.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Stop if it reblocks within days.</strong> A clog that returns quickly usually means the cable bored a hole rather than clearing the pipe, or that there is a structural problem such as a belly or a root intrusion. Repeating the same operation will produce the same result.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Do not force a cable that has stopped moving.</strong> Something is either wedged or the cable has hit a fitting it cannot negotiate. Continuing to apply force is how cables kink, snap, and end up as a second blockage that a plumber has to retrieve.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Sewage backing up is an immediate call.</strong> Standing sewage in a basement or a floor drain is a health issue as well as a plumbing one, and clearing it yourself risks contamination. This is the point at which the cost of a professional is clearly worth paying.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: Cutting Heads and Cable Choice -->
        <div id="heads" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Cutting Heads and Cable Choice
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most augers ship with one head and most people never change it, which is a shame because the head determines whether a cable clears a pipe or merely passes through it.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>The bulb or drop head is the general purpose default.</strong> A tight spiral that grabs soft material and pulls it back out. It is what comes fitted on almost every hand auger and it handles hair, soap and paper competently.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Spade and grease cutters scrape the pipe wall.</strong> A flat blade sized close to the pipe diameter shears congealed grease off the walls instead of boring a channel through it. On a kitchen line that has slowly narrowed over years, this is the head that actually restores the bore.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Root cutters are saw-toothed and for main lines only.</strong> They cut through fine root masses that have entered at a joint. They also cut anything else they meet, which is why they belong nowhere near a fixture branch or a thin-walled pipe.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Retrieving heads exist for dropped objects.</strong> A corkscrew or grabbing head is the right tool for a ring, a toy or a toothbrush, where the aim is to bring the object out intact rather than push it further along the line.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Start small and escalate.</strong> Running a bulb head first tells you where the blockage sits and what it feels like. Fitting an aggressive cutter as the opening move on an unknown pipe is how holes get made in old cast iron.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 7: Keeping Drains Clear -->
        <div id="prevention" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Keeping Drains Clear
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every blockage cleared is one that will return unless the cause changes, and the causes are consistent across almost every house.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Grease is the main kitchen culprit.</strong> Poured down a drain as a warm liquid, it congeals on the pipe wall as it cools and accumulates over months. Wiping pans with a paper towel before washing removes most of it and costs nothing.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Hair catchers solve the bathroom problem entirely.</strong> A simple mesh screen over a shower or sink drain catches the hair that causes the overwhelming majority of bathroom blockages. Emptying it weekly is far easier than snaking twice a year.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Flushable wipes are not.</strong> They do not break down the way paper does and they accumulate in bends and against roots, forming the core of blockages that then catch everything else. This is the single most common cause of main line problems in modern houses.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Hot water flushes help, chemical cleaners mostly do not.</strong> Running very hot water down a kitchen drain weekly keeps grease moving. Caustic drain cleaners generate heat, can damage older pipes, and often only bore a channel through a clog rather than removing it.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Enzyme treatments work slowly and genuinely.</strong> Biological products that digest organic matter take days rather than minutes and cannot clear an existing blockage, but used monthly they keep branch lines from accumulating in the first place.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Deal with roots before they deal with you.</strong> If a main line has ever rooted up, it will again, and the interval is usually predictable. Snaking it on a schedule before it blocks is far preferable to doing it under pressure with sewage on the floor.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 8: Frequently Asked Questions -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best drain snake?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                RIDGID PowerSpin Plus for general household use, because the autofeed and enclosed drum make it far less unpleasant to use than a bare cable. For a toilet you need a closet auger such as the RIDGID K-6P, and for a hair clog in a bathroom sink a cheap Cobra Zip-It beats both.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use a drain snake in a toilet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not a standard one. A bare steel cable scratches the glaze and can crack the bowl, which costs far more than the tool. Use a closet auger, which has a rigid curved shaft matched to the trap geometry and a vinyl guard where it touches the porcelain.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long a drain snake do I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Twenty five feet covers any sink, tub or shower blockage and reaches well into the branch line, which is the right specification for a household tool. Main line work needs fifty feet or more with a thicker cable, and that is a different category of machine entirely.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my drain keep clogging after snaking?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually because the cable bored a channel through the clog rather than clearing the pipe walls, so the remaining material rebuilds quickly. Running very hot water for several minutes afterwards helps. Repeated fast reblocking can also indicate a structural problem such as a belly or root intrusion.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a drain snake damage pipes?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, particularly in older cast iron or clay that is already thinned by corrosion or cracked by roots, where an aggressive cutting head can make a hole. Forcing a cable that has stopped moving is the other risk, as it kinks or snaps and becomes a blockage itself.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I use chemical drain cleaner or a snake?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A snake, in almost every case. Chemical cleaners frequently bore a channel rather than removing the blockage, generate heat that can damage older pipes, and leave caustic liquid sitting in the trap that will splash back when you eventually do use a cable. Never snake a drain immediately after using them.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Drain Snaking Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Drain snaking goes wrong in a small number of predictable ways, and most of them are avoidable in the first five minutes.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a Cable Snake in a Toilet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Toilet traps are tight double curves in glazed porcelain, and a bare steel cable scratches the glaze on the way in and can crack the bowl under force. A closet auger is shaped for the trap and sleeved in vinyl where it makes contact. It costs a fraction of a replacement toilet.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Snaking Straight After Chemical Cleaner</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Caustic drain cleaner that failed to clear the blockage is still sitting in the trap, and a cable disturbing it will splash it back at you. Flush thoroughly with water first, wear eye protection, and ideally do not use the chemicals in the first place.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Pushing Instead of Turning</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A cable advances by cutting and grabbing as it spins, not by being shoved. Pushing hard turns the cable into a compressed spring that kinks inside the pipe. Feed a short length at a time, keep it turning steadily, and let the head make progress on its own.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not Flushing Afterwards</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Boring a passage through a clog restores flow immediately and leaves most of the material on the pipe walls, which is why drains so often reblock within weeks. Several minutes of very hot water after clearing carries away what the cable loosened and makes the fix last.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

